            "text": "City mornings feel like a quiet rebellion against yesterday\u2019s noise. Streetlights hesitate to die, traffic whispering as if embarrassed, and the sky dons a pale lavender robe that makes even weathered brick look poetic. I slip between alleys with a thermos of coffee, my boots tapping a Morse code that only stray cats understand. There is an intimacy in watching the sun recline behind rooftops and then, almost shyly, peek over them again. The birds near the river seem to know I am spying, so they rise late, thick chords of song winding upward together. With each breath of cold air I am reminded that urban life is not the steel and glass it claims to be; it is the subtle green sprout wedged into a crumbling sidewalk, the farmer selling peaches from a cart, the old man who plants tulips by the metro exit. These small rebellions are what keep the city from being a machine.\n\nI often wonder if cities miss their own dawns while still drunk on neon. But when I stand on the bridge and watch light spill slowly over water, I feel consonant with every other observer who has ever dared to rise before the alarm. Being awake for sunrise becomes an act of defiance, a promise to notice. Sometimes the city seems like a story paused between chapters, as if the bustle could be negotiated in hushed tones. I think of suns that have risen and still remind us to look up again today."
          }
